## 11. Visible progression target

Every generated main exercise must expose one understandable current target.

Examples:

* add one repetition per set until reaching the top of the range,
* increase dumbbell load after completing all sets cleanly,
* use a slightly stronger band,
* step farther from the anchor,
* slow the lowering phase,
* hold the pause longer,
* reduce support,
* progress to the named variation,
* or maintain the current version until pain and control criteria are met.

Required rules:

* show only one primary progression action at a time;
* do not advise changing load, reps, tempo and sets simultaneously;
* fixed-weight dumbbell users must receive a non-load option;
* band progression must not rely on colour names;
* bodyweight progression must use leverage, range, control, duration or variation;
* pain-aware plans may have a maintain or recovery target rather than a demand increase;
* the target must come from canonical progression policy, not generic UI copy.

Do not redesign the progression engine.

Add the smallest stable presentation contract that exposes what the existing progression system intends.

